# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} development entry

This checkout is the application `{{PACKAGE_IDENTITY}}`. Use the source, dependency locks and public documentation shipped here; no separate maintainer repository or workstation is required for application or module development.

## Start here

Read only the task-relevant sections of [the development standard](docs/development/standard.md), then locate the relevant contract through [the documentation index](docs/README.md). Use [the quality-tool instructions](tools/quality/README.md) and each affected client's own package scripts for authorized validation. Read the affected implementation, module declarations and existing tests before changing them; reading tests does not authorize running them. Do not infer a completed capability or a passed test from a document or configuration alone.

Confirm the actual checkout, branch, worktree changes and installed dependency paths before editing. Preserve existing work and follow the application owner's current Git policy: when it requires the primary dev checkout and forbids feature branches or worktrees, work in that checkout with an exclusive write set; otherwise isolate source edits in an approved branch/worktree. Never overwrite unrelated changes or force shared history. A change request does not implicitly authorize publication, deployment, paid operations, or changes to customer data.

## Execution discipline

- Start or resume from the current requested result, unfinished work, valid evidence and running handles. Keep one owner for dependent implementation through delivery; delegate only independent work with clear write boundaries. Do not reassign stages, create extra reviewers or replay completed work merely to report progress.
- Before an expensive or mutating command, inspect its actual producer, consumers and recovery entry, registered tools, required edition/configuration, source/lock consistency and exact write set. Distinguish missing capability from unused capability or environment failure; do not discover known prerequisites by repeatedly building or installing.
- Before generation, build, upgrade or cleanup can overwrite existing work, preserve the affected tracked diff, index state, untracked file contents and base revision in a recoverable location. Hashes and path lists alone are not backups. Protect application customizations and never replace current assets with guessed reconstructions.
- Use the native entry for the final admitted module/dependency combination. Read which dependency preparation, API generation, build and compile steps it already performs; do not run those steps again separately or per module unless changed inputs or the actual contract require it. Do not add a competing runner, ledger or gate.
- On failure, retain the actual command, exit code, nested error, completed writes and recovery handle. Inspect supported failed-state forward completion and rollback separately; a rollback backup requirement does not itself forbid forward repair. Fix an evidenced cause before rerunning only the affected step; UNKNOWN or running operations must be reconciled through the same handle, not resubmitted.
- Batch known dependent commands locally with explicit failure propagation; parallelize independent reads, not shared writes. Return to model reasoning at a real decision or error, not after every mechanical command. Use existing tools rather than creating orchestration scripts for this rule.
- Read only the relevant rule and source sections. Summarize large outputs locally; normally return at most 8 KiB of selected fields and failure excerpts, keeping necessary raw evidence outside model context. At a safe milestone, compact to the current result, open issues, evidence paths and precise next action; do not reload full history or unchanged rule bodies without a reason.
- Reuse passed checks while their relevant inputs remain unchanged; report only actual coverage. Choose model and reasoning effort separately for the current uncertainty, use local tools for deterministic work, and escalate only on concrete evidence. Record one result at milestones, finish the authorized scope and stop; these rules add no test, release, deployment or data-operation permission.

## Project tool environment

Read [the project toolchain guide](docs/development/project-toolchain.md). `tools/toolchain.json` owns the project tool requirements; `.local/toolchain/host.json` privately binds this machine's installed programs and caches using `tools/toolchain.local.example.json`. Do not commit machine paths or credentials. pnpm and Composer requirements come from their existing version authorities, not duplicated guesses.

Use `python3 scripts/project-env requirements` to read requirements, explicitly `prepare` the registered local environment, then run authorized commands through `python3 scripts/project-env exec -- ...`. `doctor` is read-only and may request preparation; neither doctor nor exec installs missing tools. Preparation is not permission to test, build, start services or publish. Preserve downstream toolchain edits with the normal managed-file conflict process.

GitHub CI is optional and separately versioned through Recipes; this application does not need GitHub or a maintainer's private repository. The toolchain guide distinguishes the catalog default from a pending Recipe version. Do not overwrite existing workflows or Recipe baselines to adopt an update.

## Source and dependency boundaries

For management UI changes, first read [the UI development guidelines](docs/development/ui-guidelines.md). Reuse Element Plus and existing Page components; preserve Shell themes and application overrides. Ant Design is a design reference, not an additional component or stylesheet dependency.

The backend application and business modules live under `server/`; `web`, `platform`, `pc` and `uniapp` are distinct clients when included in this application profile. Module dependencies, public capabilities, routes and frontend contributions come from the checked-in declarations. Cross-module calls use explicitly exported operations or stable results, not another module's internal Models, repositories or private tables. For non-business behavior, reference Core or scaffold standard implementations and use their existing configuration, bindings and extension points; retain genuine application business customizations. Reuse the existing container, ORM, authorization, errors, tasks and upgrade mechanism rather than introducing parallel frameworks.

**Module-first ownership:** Implement new business tables, migrations, domain services, APIs, permissions, menus and client pages as an application-owned Module; use an existing official Module through its public contract when it already owns that business. Reuse complete business capabilities by distributing Modules, not by putting their services into the technical Core or copying them between applications. Keep the scaffold host for bootstrap, native binding, Module composition, installation and upgrades; do not modify host `app/common`, `AppService` or managed scaffold files for an ordinary business feature. When a demonstrated technical default is missing, improve the appropriate PHP/Web Core and adopt it through its public API or native binding rather than adding an application-local alternative. Application-specific differences belong in app-owned Modules, configuration and existing override points; preserve managed upstream files through the native conflict-aware upgrade.

For tenant-admin, platform and module management pages, follow the **管理内容区与页面布局** section in [the shipped development standard](docs/development/standard.md). Prefer the existing `@peanut-admin/ui-vue` page structure and Element Plus controls; do not rebuild page shells, global UI overrides or parallel component libraries. Select list, detail, form, workbench or complex-workspace patterns to fit the actual workflow, retaining business operations and authorization in their owning module. Verify changed pages in a real browser at desktop and narrow widths; record NOT_RUN when browser access is unavailable. Use the existing conflict-aware scaffold upgrade to preserve customer changes to AGENTS, pages and styles.

Install dependencies with the relevant native lock and declared package manager before validating code. Missing dependencies or an unexpected source path must be reported, not silently replaced with a different version or a developer's global installation. Development checks in `tools/quality` are separate from runtime dependencies. Do not commit installed `vendor`, `node_modules`, caches, secrets or build logs.

## Changes, checks and upgrades

For request-chain changes, read `python3 scripts/read-development-docs request-cache-contract` and trace the actual read and invalidation owners. Reuse ThinkPHP Model/Query/pagination/Cache and the existing identity, permission and data-scope contracts. Measure the real HTTP path before optimizing; avoid per-row queries, full-detail identity reads and full-table display aggregation. Keep low-frequency-write summaries in the owning module's transaction.

Choose the smallest native workflow from `docs/public/module-development.md`: ordinary PHP method edits do not require release packaging, dependency installation, API generation or client builds. Run those producers only when their inputs change. Use selected installed modules and `--prepared-release` after necessary preparation; use `module sync` only within its admitted same-package identity. A scaffold upgrade is plan → resolve actual conflicts → apply; the CLI already verifies it. Preserve tracked/index/untracked work before writes, retain the recovery handle, and do not repeat whole-product tests or a full release for a small edit.

Before a formal candidate, settle the selected module sources, versions, native dependencies and module lock, then refresh affected API, inventory and other derived outputs through the existing producers. Verify their versions and source digests against the final inputs; do not repeat steps already performed by the native entry or repackage after every small fix. Module-producing applications upgrade the scaffold first, then review and optimize their modules before independently releasing them under the applicable authorization. Consuming applications upgrade the scaffold first, then the modules they actually use and their native dependency locks, followed by migrations, builds and business confirmation required by the changes. A scaffold upgrade is not a module upgrade.

Keep development, local packaging and authorized checks, fixed-input qualification, formal tag publication, and application adoption/first installation separate. Confirm the local result before scheduling formal publication; reuse valid evidence and qualify only missing or invalidated scope, within the actual native entry's capabilities. Formal publication consumes the fixed qualified originals; it does not restart development, rebuild/repack them or rerun qualification tests. Required publisher tool preparation, digest checks and public-channel locked-consumer confirmation remain distinct. A dirty development package or identity-only comparison is not formal qualification. Current upstream Core tag workflows consume their selected successful CI qualification artifacts; the scaffold requires successful CI and template qualification for the exact source. Arbitrary local-package admission and partial qualification reuse are not implied; report unsupported paths instead of inventing a runner or gate. Upstream maintainers use [the Code release contract](https://github.com/peanut-business/peanut-admin-code/blob/main/docs/development/core-release.md); applications do not need to execute that process. Publication does not authorize upgrading other applications; public-channel first-install confirmation is a separate later stage, not a completed pre-publication check.

Preserve the documented identity, tenant, permission, transaction, soft-delete, file and error contracts. Keep source, templates, imports, types and public documentation consistent. When signatures, context or schemas change, inspect direct constructors, container bindings and existing fixtures as affected; reading them does not authorize test maintenance or execution. Authorized fixtures must match the current schema and required state; cache behavior uses the configured native driver and the formal write/invalidation entry, not direct database writes that bypass it. Regenerate affected API/SDK data and module artifacts through their existing tools; never edit checksums to make a check pass. Use the applicable configured formatter and necessary compile/type checks; run positive/negative behavior tests only within the authorized test scope. Report the actual source and dependency identity, command, exit status, failures and work not executed. Parts skipped by fail-fast remain NOT_RUN; report environment/dependency failures separately from product assertions. Static or synthetic tests do not prove live database, browser or recovery behavior.

`.peanut/application-manifest.json` records managed and application-owned paths and baseline digests. Do not mark an entire application or client as custom to prevent upstream fixes. New customer modules and pages remain customer work; local changes to managed files require the documented conflict-aware upgrade process. Review conflicts against the real baseline, application changes and target implementation; do not overwrite customizations or fabricate baseline hashes to suppress conflicts. This shared entry is managed with the same baseline protection; do not overwrite local edits during an upgrade.

Use the shipped installation instructions for a first installation and the `scripts/scaffold-upgrade` flow for upstream source changes and `scripts/deploy-update.sh` for product Git updates. Do not re-run first-install initialization as an upgrade, execute unverified target-package code, or replace real upgrade/recovery checks with a fresh database. Inspect `resources/project-resources.json` and the actual authorization before running services or accessing a database. Coordinate conflicting writes and use native transaction/migration locks; require exclusivity only where the actual operation or qualification contract requires it, not merely because connections share a database. Never borrow another project's resource or erase existing data to simplify a test.
